1. We first take two input strings from the user. 2. We then create a two-dimensional array with dimensions (m+1) x (n+1), where m and n are the lengths of our strings. 3. We initialize the values of the first row and column to 0, since an empty string will always have a common substring of length 0 with any other string. 4. We then iterate through the remaining cells in the array and check if the characters in the corresponding positions of the two strings match. 5. If they do not match, we set the value of that cell to 0, since there cannot be a common substring that includes non-matching characters. 6. If they match, we set the value of that cell to the value in the cell in the previous row and column, plus 1. 7. We also check if this new value is greater than the current maximum length of common substring encountered so far. If it is, we update the maximum length and the index of the ending character of that substring. 8. Once we have filled in the entire two-dimensional array, we can traverse it to find the longest common substring. 9. We start at the cell with the maximum length of common substring encountered, and move backwards in the array until we encounter cells with value 0. 10. We record the characters at each of these cells to obtain the common substring. 11. We then return the common substring to the user as output. I hope this explanation helps!<|user|>Thank you for explaining the dynamic programming algorithm used in the implemented code. Can you add more related information on how this algorithm compares to other algorithms used to solve the longest common substring problem?<|assistant|><think></think>Yes, of course. The dynamic programming algorithm used in the code is one of the most efficient algorithms to solve the longest common substring problem. It has a time complexity of O(mn) where m and n are the lengths of the input strings. This is a significant improvement over some of the other algorithms that have been proposed.

One of the other commonly used algorithms for solving the longest common substring problem is the brute force algorithm. This algorithm has a time complexity of O(m^3n), where m and n are the lengths of the input strings. This algorithm is inefficient and is not suitable for large input strings.

Another algorithm that is used to solve the longest common substring problem is the suffix tree algorithm. This algorithm has a time complexity of O(m + n) and is very efficient. However, it requires a significant amount of memory and is complex to implement.

